Kaspersky describes httpMalice as a PebbleDash-based backdoor (HTTP or HTTPS in version 1.9, the Dropbox API in version 1.8), MemLoad as a loader that reflectively runs httpTroy after anti-VM checks, and httpTroy as the long-term access and exfiltration backdoor, rather than an HTTP-only loader, a reflective DLL loader and a C2 backdoor. The report gives observations, not detection guidance, and its targets are mainly South Korean, with PebbleDash victims including Brazilian defense organizations and a German defense firm. HelloDoor is an early-stage Rust backdoor whose TryCloudflare-hosted C2 makes its infrastructure hard to trace.
